The assembly at the city's war memorial included former prisoner of war (PoW) Len Gibson, who was captured in Singapore and compelled to labor on the Burma Death Railway. A separate commemoration occurred at the Burma Star Memorial located at St Thomas' Church in Newcastle. In London, a remembrance service was officiated by the Queen and the Duke of Edinburgh. Mr. Gibson, aged 95, expressed his appreciation for the continued recognition of historical events, stating that the experiences from that period remain indelibly etched in his memory, as daily occurrences often evoke recollections of those times. He recounted that their Japanese captors subjected them to severe mistreatment, likening their condition to slavery. The prisoners were beaten if they failed to meet labor expectations, and despite their willingness to work diligently, they suffered from starvation due to inadequate nourishment.

Graham Hall, chairman of the Sunderland Armed Forces Network and organizer of the memorial event, emphasized the necessity of ongoing remembrance. He articulated that such commemorations serve as a potent reminder of the persistent inhumanity that humanity inflicts upon itself. Hall further noted that Sunderland boasts the highest concentration of veterans relative to any other city in the country, with a veteran population exceeding 26,500. He conjectured that it is unlikely for many families to remain unaffected by military service in some form.

